<BaseLayout title="Cooling System" description="Radiator, fan clearance, fan relays, Holley fan control, steam vent routing, IAT placement, and heater hoses for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Cooling System</h1>
  <p>
    LS engines make a lot of heat in a tight bay. Plan radiator capacity, fan strategy, and oil cooling early — especially on BK1 cars with less front clearance.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc">
    <a href="#clearance">Clearance</a>
    <a href="#radiator">Radiator</a>
    <a href="#fans">Fans &amp; relays</a>
    <a href="#stock-relays">Stock fan relays</a>
    <a href="#key-on">Key-on fans</a>
    <a href="#iat">IAT sensor</a>
    <a href="#heater-hose">Heater hose</a>
    <a href="#steam">Steam vent</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="clearance">Before You Start — Clearance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>BK1:</strong> get a Corvette-spacing front accessory drive. You can get away with F-body (middle) spacing, but you want as much room as possible between the engine and the radiator fans.</li>
    <li><strong>BK2:</strong> much more front-end clearance — about 1.5" more.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="fan-clearance-ls.jpg" alt="Tape measure between LS accessory drive and radiator" caption="Measuring engine-to-radiator clearance." />
    <Figure src="fan-clearance-reference.jpg" alt="Tape measure reference across front of engine bay" caption="Clearance reference measurement." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="radiator">Radiator &amp; Oil Cooler</h2>
  <p>
    Run a 3.8 upgraded radiator with a fan shroud. Jason Riddle makes them — reach out to him for one.
    To keep temps down you can also try a push/pull setup: most run 2 puller fans behind the radiator, and some add 1 or 2 pusher fans in front.
  </p>
  <p>An oil cooler is advised to help control oil temps.</p>
  <Figure src="dual-spal-fans.jpg" alt="Dual SPAL fans on aluminum shroud installed" caption="Dual SPAL fans on an aluminum shroud." />
  <p>
    Example fan/shroud: <a href="https://www.ebay.com/itm/291874634006" target="_blank" rel="noopener">12" HP SPAL fans on aluminum shroud with louvers (26" x 19.5")</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Radiator Hoses</h3>
  <p><em>Future addition.</em></p>

  <h2 id="fans">Radiator Fans &amp; Relays</h2>
  <p>3 OEM radiator fan wires:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Harness wire</th><th>Function</th><th>Fan-side color</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Red</td><td>High side</td><td>Black/Red</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Green</td><td>Low side</td><td>Black/White</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Black</td><td>Ground</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    I cut open the middle of the wiring harness at the firewall and pulled the thick red/green wires back toward the battery, enough to reach the new fan relays.
    The Kiazen relays don't need a 12V trigger lead since they're solid state, but with a traditional relay you'll need a fuse tap to a 12V key-on source for both relays.
    Wire in 2 relays, one per fan. The Holley <strong>gray/yellow (fan #1)</strong> and <strong>gray/red (fan #2)</strong> trigger wires go to <strong>pin 85</strong> as triggered ground.
  </p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="fan-wiring-bulkhead.jpg" alt="Red and green factory fan wires pulled from firewall bulkhead" caption="Factory fan wires pulled from the firewall harness." />
    <Figure src="fan-controllers-holley.jpg" alt="Fan 1 and fan 2 relays mounted near battery" caption="Fan #1 / #2 relays mounted near the battery." />
    <Figure src="holley-fan-pin-map.jpg" alt="Holley Terminator X ECU pin map with custom fan outputs" caption="Holley pin map — fan outputs." />
    <Figure src="holley-custom-fan1-triggers.jpg" alt="Holley custom fan 1 input and sensor triggers" caption="Holley Custom Fan 1 trigger settings." />
  </div>

  <h3 id="stock-relays">Using the Stock Fan Relays</h3>
  <p>
    In the passenger-side fuse box, the two white wires with a black stripe are power to the driver's-side fan high/low relays — apply 12V key-on power here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each year/model ECU connector has a different pinout. The green (high) and blue (low) wires should be your fan relay ground triggers; these go to the Holley ECU fan wires.
  </p>

  <h3 id="key-on">Stop Fans Running at Key-On</h3>
  <p>
    To keep Holley from kicking the fans on until after the car has started:
    <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dzDAmxhpKDA&t=615s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terminator X Tuning — Disable Key-On Fan Operation</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="iat">IAT Sensor</h2>
  <p>
    With the radiator blowing hot air directly at the IAT sensor I was getting wildly incorrect air temp readings. I originally put mine in the 90° coupler at the throttle body
    since that's where the Holley harness reached and there's no spot for it in the FAST 102mm intake manifold.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've now moved the IAT to the back of the FAST 102, tapped for <strong>3/8" NPT</strong>. This requires grinding down the tab that's in the way.
  </p>
  <Figure src="iat-sensor-location.jpg" alt="IAT sensor tapped into back of FAST 102 intake manifold" caption="IAT relocated to the back of the FAST 102." />

  <h2 id="steam">Steam Vent Line (Don't Skip This)</h2>
  <p>
    The LS thermostat sits low in the water pump housing, so air gets trapped at the top of the heads. The steam ports bleed that air off.
    Without them you get hot spots, which can lead to overheating and detonation.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Connect the steam ports (two at the front on later engines, four on early ones) and run the common line to a port <strong>just below the radiator cap</strong>.</li>
    <li>Don't route it to the overflow port. Routing it to the water pump is debated: LSX Mag lists a tapped water pump port as an option, but Motion Raceworks says the pump just recirculates the steam.</li>
    <li>Many aftermarket swap radiators don't have a steam fitting. Check before you buy, or have one welded in.</li>
    <li>An engine that sits level (common in swaps) benefits from a four-corner steam kit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="heater-hose">Heater Hose</h2>
  <p>
    The heater core inlet is on the driver's side and the outlet is on the passenger side. 3/4" hose fits great on both.
    The hose below was cut on the short end to fit behind the heads — use adapters and extend it to the pump.
    The driver's-side port goes to the smaller rear port on the pump; the passenger-side port is the return to the front port on the pump.
  </p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="heater-core-ports-labeled.jpg" alt="Heater core inlet and outlet ports labeled at firewall" caption="Heater core inlet/outlet labeled." />
    <Figure src="heater-hose-firewall-closeup.jpg" alt="Heater hoses connected at firewall" caption="Hoses at the firewall." />
    <Figure src="heater-hose-routing-engine.jpg" alt="Heater hose routing along LS engine" caption="Routing behind the heads to the pump." />
    <Figure src="heater-hose-90deg-product.jpg" alt="90 degree 3/4 inch heater hose product listing" caption="The 90° hose used." />
  </div>
  <p>
    <a href="https://a.co/d/dILkNUc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Swap heater hose 90° bend + 2 clamps, 3/4" ID (19mm) 5" x 25" for LS LQ4/LQ9</a>
  </p>

